From: WelsonJS
Subject: [PATCH] BitNet I2_S: build without the BitNet repository and fix the fork

Everything for the BitNet I2_S type of this fork: building it without the
BitNet repository (and on Windows with GGML_BACKEND_DL), correct results on
CPUs without AVX2, and microsoft/BitNet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf working as a chat
model.

1. ggml : build without the BitNet repository unless TL1/TL2 is on

  This fork is the llama.cpp submodule of microsoft/BitNet and uses files from
  the BitNet repository, which are not in the fork:

    - ../../../../src/ggml-bitnet-lut.cpp and ../../../../include (ggml-bitnet.h,
      bitnet-lut-kernels.h): only used by the TL1/TL2 lookup-table kernels
      (GGML_BITNET_ARM_TL1, GGML_BITNET_X86_TL2). Every use of ggml-bitnet.h is
      already inside #if defined(GGML_BITNET_ARM_TL1) ||
      defined(GGML_BITNET_X86_TL2), but the CMake files add these paths
      unconditionally. Add them only when TL1 or TL2 is on.
    - gemm-config.h: the block sizes of the I2_S kernels (ggml-cpu-i2s.c), which
      are part of the fork. Copy it from BitNet
      0b341e582afbf9e1011f24744b554c96a3477eb5 into ggml/src/ggml-cpu, next to
      ggml-cpu-i2s.c, and add defaults for CPU variants that reach none of its
      cases (the baseline x64 variant of GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS, and MSVC
      variants below AVX, since MSVC does not define __SSSE3__); the non-AVX2
      path of ggml-cpu-i2s.c uses ROW_BLOCK_SIZE and COL_BLOCK_SIZE.

  With TL1/TL2 off the fork then builds on its own (the "Clang or GCC is
  required" check is in BitNet's CMake, not in the fork).

2. ggml : move the I2_S reference (de)quantization to ggml-base

  ggml.c (ggml-base) uses dequantize_row_i2_s (the I2_S type traits) and
  quantize_i2_s (ggml_quantize_chunk), but the fork defines both in
  ggml-cpu/quants.c, which is part of the CPU backend. That links in a static
  build, and in shared builds on ELF platforms where undefined symbols are
  resolved at load time. With GGML_BACKEND_DL on Windows each CPU variant is a
  separate DLL that ggml-base.dll cannot link against, and the link fails with
  LNK2001/LNK2019 for these two symbols.

  Both functions are plain scalar C. Move them to ggml-quants.c and declare
  them GGML_API, so the CPU backend (ops.cpp uses dequantize_row_i2_s) imports
  them from ggml-base like the other reference quantization functions.

3. ggml-cpu : do not use src1_cont outside the llamafile block

  The fork's I2_S GEMV/GEMM fast path in ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat uses
  src1_cont, which is declared only inside the "#if GGML_USE_LLAMAFILE" block
  above it. ggml-cpu.c undefines GGML_USE_LLAMAFILE when the target has
  __ARM_FEATURE_SVE or __ARM_FEATURE_MATMUL_INT8, so an ARM64 build with
  -march=armv8.7-a (the Windows ARM64 toolchain, cmake/arm64-windows-llvm.cmake)
  fails:

    ggml-cpu.c:1501:40: error: use of undeclared identifier 'src1_cont'

  x86 builds do not see it because GGML_USE_LLAMAFILE stays defined there.
  Call ggml_is_contiguous(src1) directly, which is what src1_cont holds.

4. ggml-cpu : fix the non-AVX2 I2_S fallbacks

  The I2_S kernels are correct only on their AVX2 path. On CPUs without AVX2
  (and in the x64, sse42 and sandybridge variants of GGML_CPU_ALL_VARIANTS)
  microsoft/BitNet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf generated only "?" tokens:

    - ggml_vec_dot_i2_i8_s (quants.c): the scalar fallback read the 2-bit
      codes sequentially, while I2_S interleaves 128 weights per 32 bytes
      (byte gp holds weights gp, gp+32, gp+64, gp+96 in bits 7-6, 5-4, 3-2,
      1-0, as in dequantize_row_i2_s); it mapped the codes to -1/0/+1, while
      the callers subtract the activation sum themselves (the AVX2 code uses the
      unsigned codes 0/1/2); and it addressed row r at r * (n/4) instead of
      r * bx / 4. Rewrite it to compute what the AVX2 code computes.
    - ggml_gemm_i2_i8_s (ggml-cpu-i2s.c): with ACT_PARALLEL (set by
      gemm-config.h) the fallback called ggml_vec_dot_i2_i8_s as if it iterated
      over activation columns with an output stride; it iterates over weight
      rows and writes them consecutively. Call it once per activation column,
      which gives the AVX2 code's layout s[col * bs + row].

  With both changes the x64 variant generates the same tokens as the AVX2
  variants.

5. models : use squared ReLU for bitnet-b1.58 (BitNet b1.58 2B-4T)

  The fork registers bitnet-b1.58, the architecture of
  microsoft/BitNet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf, as a subclass of bitnet and builds both
  with the bitnet graph, whose FFN uses SiLU. The 2B-4T model uses squared ReLU
  (hidden_act "relu2" in microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T config.json); the older
  bitnet_b1_58 models (bitnet) use SiLU.

  With SiLU the 2B-4T model answers the first sentence and then never emits
  <|eot_id|>, repeating itself until the token limit; F16 weights converted
  from the same file gave the same tokens, so the I2_S kernels were not the
  cause. With squared ReLU it answers and stops with <|eot_id|>.

6. llama : use the original chat template for BitNet b1.58 2B-4T

  microsoft/BitNet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf embeds the chat template
  "Human: ...\n\nBITNETAssistant: ", which the model does not follow: through
  /v1/chat/completions it invents a conversation instead of answering. The
  original model (microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T, tokenizer_config.json) uses
  "User: ...<|eot_id|>Assistant: ".

  Like the existing one-off fix for Mistral-Small-2503 in the same function,
  return the original template when the architecture is bitnet-b1.58 and the
  embedded template contains "BITNETAssistant". A template given with
  --chat-template or --chat-template-file still takes precedence.

7. llama : use the llama-bpe pre-tokenizer for BitNet b1.58 2B-4T

  microsoft/BitNet-b1.58-2B-4T-gguf has no tokenizer.ggml.pre, so llama.cpp
  falls back to the "default" pre-tokenizer and warns that generation quality
  will be degraded. The original model (microsoft/bitnet-b1.58-2B-4T) uses the
  Llama 3 tokenizer. The two split text differently: for example "I'm",
  "they'll", "won't" and blank lines give different tokens.

  When the architecture is bitnet-b1.58 and tokenizer.ggml.pre is missing, use
  "llama-bpe". A file that sets tokenizer.ggml.pre, or --override-kv
  tokenizer.ggml.pre=..., is not affected.

Apply with: git -c core.autocrlf=false apply <this file>

